<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vaishya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as vaiśya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vaishya is the third of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a> of the Vedic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> social order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, ranked below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriyas</a>, whom it traditionally had the duty to sustain or patronise. Hindu texts, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>, assigned Vaishyas agriculture, the care of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and trade, but over time they came to be landowners, traders and money-lenders. With the Brahmins and Kshatriyas they claim dvija, &quot;twice-born&quot;, status after the sacrament of initiation. The historian Ram Sharan Sharma described the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> as a Vaishya dynasty.</p></div>
